<Project>
  <!--
    /wd5054 - workaround for Qt 5.15.2 + modern MSVC (/std:c++20).

    WHAT FAILS
    ----------
    Qt 5.15.2's QtWidgets/qsizepolicy.h contains an inline body that mixes
    enumeration types (around lines 137-139, in expandingDirections()):

        warning C5054: operator '&': deprecated between enumerations
                      of different types

    TreatWarningAsError is true for Release|* and Static|*, so the warning
    becomes error C2220, the pch.cpp compile dies, the precompiled header is
    never established, and the run then reports a cascade of confusing
    secondary errors ("missing type specifier" in DebugConsole.h / Process.h,
    a spurious "operator== already declared") that are parse corruption from
    the dead PCH, not real breakage.

    WHY pch.h's OWN PRAGMA DOES NOT CATCH IT
    ----------------------------------------
    pch.h already wraps its Qt includes in #pragma warning(push) /
    warning(disable: 5054) / warning(pop), and it is not enough. Per the MSVC
    docs on precompiled headers, under /Yu (Use Precompiled Header File) "the
    compiler ignores all preprocessor directives (including pragmas) that
    appear in the source code that will be precompiled". Because pch.h's guard
    is a balanced push/pop, the disable is already undone by the end of the
    PCH, so any instance of the warning emitted in a consuming translation
    unit is unguarded. The docs also state the fix explicitly: "To override
    the precompiled header warning options, set another warning option on the
    command line" - which is why this lives in a props file that feeds the
    compiler, not in a source pragma.

    WHY A COMMAND-LINE /wd RATHER THAN /Wv
    --------------------------------------
    /Wv:<version> suppresses every warning introduced after that compiler
    generation. /Wv:18 (VS2015 RTM) would silence essentially every MSVC
    warning added in the last decade - including in this repo's own code -
    which the docs explicitly advise against ("Don't suppress all new warnings
    permanently"). /wd5054 disables exactly the one warning, compile-wide,
    keeps every other modern warning active, and survives the PCH state.

    WHY HERE RATHER THAN AN EDIT TO THE .vcxproj
    --------------------------------------------
    AdditionalOptions is item metadata on ClCompile, not an MSBuild property,
    so the flag cannot be passed on the MSBuild command line.
    Directory.Build.props is imported automatically by Microsoft.Common.props
    for every C++ project under this folder (import order per MSLearn:
    vcxproj -> Microsoft.Cpp.default.props -> Microsoft.Common.props ->
    Directory.Build.props -> Microsoft.Cpp.props ...), so all three projects
    pick it up, no existing file is edited, and CI matches a local build.

    REMOVE THIS FILE WHEN
    ---------------------
      * the project moves off Qt 5.15.x (end-of-life; the offending header
        cannot be fixed in a prebuilt binary), or
      * TreatWarningAsError is turned off for Release|* / Static|*.

    If another warning from a Qt header surfaces (the pragma above is inert, so
    one more may yet appear), add the narrow /wd<n> here rather than reaching
    for /Wv: the cost of one more specific disable is far lower than losing all
    post-VS2015 warnings.
  -->
  <ItemDefinitionGroup>
    <ClCompile>
      <AdditionalOptions>%(AdditionalOptions) /wd5054</AdditionalOptions>
    </ClCompile>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>
</Project>
